What this project is
--------------------

A decoder-only Transformer written from scratch in PyTorch, applied to chess: the
model reads a position and a candidate move and predicts the Stockfish 16 win
probability of that move, discretised into buckets. There is no search in the model
itself; an alpha-beta layer on top of the network and a Lichess bot are separate
components of this repository.


Reimplementations of DeepMind's searchless_chess
------------------------------------------------

This project is not a fork of, and contains no copied source from, DeepMind's
searchless_chess. It does reimplement specific design choices from it, deliberately
and for comparability of the reported numbers:

  searchless_chess
  Copyright 2024 DeepMind Technologies Limited
  Licensed under the Apache License, Version 2.0
  https://github.com/google-deepmind/searchless_chess

The pieces that are reimplementations rather than original work:

  * The FEN tokenizer in src/chess_data.py: the same 77-token fixed-length encoding
    over the same 31-symbol vocabulary.
  * The action space in src/chess_data.py: the 1968 UCI moves, generated in the same
    order, so action indices line up with the reference implementation.
  * The bucketed win-probability target in src/chess_data.py: the Stockfish win
    probability is discretised into uniform bins and learned as a classification
    problem rather than regressed.
  * The record layout of the ChessBench .bag files, decoded in src/chess_data.py
    without the Apache Beam dependency.
  * The puzzle scoring rule in src/puzzles_eval.py: the solver plays the odd plies of
    the puzzle line, every solver move must be found, and any move that mates in one
    is accepted. This matches searchless_chess/src/puzzles.py so that accuracies are
    comparable.

Everything else in this repository -- the Transformer implementation, the training
loops, the Triton kernel, the alpha-beta search, the evaluation harnesses, the
baselines and the Lichess bot -- was written for this project.


Datasets
--------

Training and evaluation use the ChessBench dataset released with searchless_chess
(action_value shards: one record per (position, legal move) pair, annotated by
Stockfish 16), fetched at run time from
https://storage.googleapis.com/searchless_chess/data/.

Puzzle accuracy is measured on Lichess puzzles. The Lichess open databases, including
the puzzle and evaluation databases (https://database.lichess.org), are released under
the Creative Commons CC0 1.0 Universal public domain dedication.

No dataset file is committed to this repository: data/ and checkpoints/ are ignored by
.gitignore, and downloaded copies live locally under data/chessbench/ (ChessBench
action-value shards and puzzles.csv) and data/lichess/ (lichess_db_puzzle.csv.zst).
The only chess data in version control is a single Lichess puzzle (id 000Zo) inlined
as a FEN and move list in tests/test_chess_data.py, used as a test fixture.
